NAME
    pkg - manage packages in the m package subsystem

SYNOPSIS
    pkg install <package-spec>...
    pkg uninstall <package-spec>...
    pkg versions <package-class>
    pkg default [-u] [--] <package-spec>
    pkg provider default <facility> [<provider-selector>]
    pkg provider default -u [--] <facility>
    pkg provider bind <consumer> <facility> [<provider-selector>]
    pkg provider bind -u [--] <consumer> <facility>

DESCRIPTION
    pkg is the public package command of the technical m layer. Package catalog
    data is obtained from the current pkg-catalog source; installed package content
    is managed beneath $m_PKG_DIR.

    Package operations use the real package pipeline for catalog/repository
    resolution, artifact integrity, download, extraction, integration, defaults,
    and launch/state integration as applicable.

PACKAGE OPERANDS
    Package operands use the current forms:

        <package>
        <package>@<version>
        <package>!<osarch>
        <package>@<version>!<osarch>

    A version or osarch suffix is optional where the selected subcommand permits
    it. When installation omits a version, pkg resolves the applicable version from
    the current catalog for the requested package/platform class.

    Provider selectors use the same four package-spec shapes. An omitted version
    expresses a provider-package selection that follows that package's current
    default when resolved; an explicit version expresses a pinned provider version.

COMMANDS
    install <package-spec>...
        Install one or more requested packages through the current catalog and
        package integration pipeline. Processing is best-effort per operand: a
        failed operand emits an error and does not prevent later independently
        installable operands from being attempted.

        An already installed concrete is not reinstalled. Its error diagnostic
        identifies the installed concrete and the current/default concrete for
        the same package/platform class when one exists.

        $m_PKG_DIR is materialized on demand when the first syntactically valid
        install operand is processed. An invocation containing only invalid
        package operands does not create the package store.

    uninstall <package-spec>...
        Remove one or more installed package versions. When a version is omitted,
        the current/default version is selected when available; a single installed
        version may also be resolved directly.

    versions <package-class>
        List installed concrete package identities for one package/platform class.
        A version suffix is not accepted for this query.

    default <package-spec>
        With an explicit version, select that installed version as the current
        default for the package/platform class. Without a version, print the
        current concrete default identity.

    default -u [--] <package-class>
        Clear the current default binding for the package/platform class. A version
        suffix is not accepted when -u is used.

    provider default <facility>
        Print the configured system default provider selector for the facility.

    provider default <facility> <provider-selector>
        Set the system default provider selector for the facility. Commands declared
        by that facility are reconciled into bin/ext or bin/ext-<osarch> according
        to provider-selector and package-default semantics. Every later m bootstrap
        also re-resolves the facility default and applies the selected provider's
        facility-env projection when that provider is resolvable for the active
        platform class. Already-running processes are unchanged.

    provider default -u [--] <facility>
        Remove the configured system default provider selector and its owned global
        facility command projection.

    provider bind <consumer> <facility>
        Print the configured provider selector bound to the consumer/facility pair.

    provider bind <consumer> <facility> <provider-selector>
        Set the provider selector bound to the consumer/facility pair.

    provider bind -u [--] <consumer> <facility>
        Remove the consumer binding. Absence of a binding means that dependency
        resolution inherits the system facility default.

STATE
    Mutable package state is resolved through state-path rather than reconstructed
    by callers. Package HOME and configuration used at launch are user-scoped state.

    RumiAI-managed package configuration normally uses the reserved .m subnamespace
    inside the package conf area. Provider bindings are the explicit exception:
    a system-scoped consumer binding is stored as binding/<facility> in that
    package's system conf area and contains exactly one provider selector.

FILES
    $m_PKG_DIR
        Managed package store root. pkg install creates this directory on demand
        when the first syntactically valid install operand is processed.

EXIT STATUS
    0   The requested operation succeeded.
    1   The requested package operation could not be completed, at least one
        install operand failed in a best-effort batch, or the requested installed
        class/default could not be resolved where required. Successful operands in
        a partially failed install batch remain installed.
    2   The command or subcommand invocation itself is invalid. For pkg install,
        an invalid package operand inside a non-empty batch is reported per
        operand and contributes to status 1 rather than aborting the batch.

    Individual package execution launched after integration has its own exit status;
    that is not the exit status of this pkg management command.

EXAMPLES
    Install the catalog-selected version of a package:

        pkg install nodejs

    List installed versions for the current platform class:

        pkg versions nodejs

    Query the current default concrete identity:

        pkg default nodejs

    Clear the default binding:

        pkg default -u nodejs

    Select Temurin as the system provider for the java facility:

        pkg provider default java temurin

    Pin Maven to a specific Temurin version:

        pkg provider bind maven java temurin@25.0.4.1+1

